WEBVTT

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:07.430
Between the Earth and the Moon there are five places where a spacecraft can almost stand still,

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:10.350
where gravity and motion balance.

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:19.360
Joseph-Louis Lagrange found them with pen and paper in 1772.

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:27.420
In 2010, two NASA probes called ARTEMIS became the first spacecraft to orbit Earth–Moon Lagrange points.

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:35.330
Since 2022, the James Webb Space Telescope has worked near a Lagrange point of the Sun and the Earth.

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:42.640
These points are balanced, but not stable: like a ball resting on a saddle.

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:49.880
So a spacecraft circles them on a halo orbit, and fires small thrusts every few weeks to stay.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:59.120
Webb works 1.5 million kilometres from Earth, behind a 6.5-metre golden mirror.

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:06.030
Now compare your design: less fuel spent getting there means a longer life once you arrive.

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:11.470
<v Roberto>Chaos is not the enemy. It is the rule we play by.

